# The vision comes first

Full statement: `docs/THESIS.md`. Read it before any slice that changes what the player sees or does. This rule is the operative summary; when they differ, the thesis is right.

## The one sentence

**Habitat is a living sand castle.** You build a form out of the substrates nature gives you, then you choose the forces at work on it, run time forward, and see what became of it — including what life moved in and what the water took.

Origin: terrain editing in RCT3 sandbox, and wanting water to trickle down a sculpted mountain and pool. That is the thing being rebuilt, at simulation depth.

## The loop

```
build the form → choose the forces → run time → look → change the forces → run again
```

- **Phase one is sculpting.** Tactile and **abundant** — RCT3, not rationed (C-006). Terrain is a cause; ecosystems are never painted (N-001).
- **Phase two is not more sculpting.** The post-build verb is **controlling which forces act and how hard** — rain regime, erosion, fire, season, time (C-004). This axis is *not* the same as spatial siting, which is all the register historically recorded.
- **Running time is the instrument**, not a convenience. Fast-forward is the tide coming in.
- **Then run it again differently.** Same castle, more rain (C-005).

## The two payoffs

1. **Nature takes what you built** — berms slump, channels incise, pools silt. Already implemented (Slice 8) and still invisible to a player.
2. **Life moves into it** — something arrives *because the conditions suit it*, not because it was introduced (C-007). The limiting-factor / HSI spine is the **arrival gate**, not an inspector layer.

The castle coming alive is the good ending: a vegetated berm meets the next storm differently than a bare one. Slice 6 + Slice 8 already do this — it has never been shown.

## "Restoration" means founding, not repair

Set something up that will thrive; the test is whether it carries on without you (G-005 persistence window). Not damage-to-baseline. Where docs say "restoration," read founding.

## What this rules out

- Force control that targets a place — you set the rainfall, you site an ignition; you never drag a storm onto a hill you dislike. Regimes and pulses, not smiting.
- Rationing sculpting. Scarcity lives in ecological time (RC-004), not in an action economy.
- Progression gates that withhold tools until the player "understands" — sandbox stays open (G-001).
- Any payoff that only exists in the inspector. If the world cannot show it, it is not done.

## The standard

**The 20-second clip test** (THESIS §8): could you record twenty seconds — build, run time, watch nature have its way with it — that reads to a stranger with no explanation and makes them want to try it? When the answer is no, the next work is whatever moves that clip closest to existing, and **it is almost never a new system**.

Use it to order work. It costs no owner session.

## Where the open questions live

Register §16.5 candidates **C-004**…**C-009** are the thesis's unresolved edges: force control, branch-and-compare, abundant sculpting, arrival vs introduction, response latency, substrate differentiation. They are **Open** — implement under them as hypotheses, never as settled policy, and never invent a tenth without filing it.
